Derived from upstream commit cf99fc252a44e3f36763abe1db8744757f1b0297
(external_plugins/discord, plugin version 0.0.4).

Modifications by IbbyLabs, per Apache-2.0 section 4(b):

  - The read tools (fetch_messages, search_messages, list_forum_threads,
    list_forum_tags, list_members, describe_server) accept chat_id in place of
    channel, and name the missing parameter themselves when neither is given.
    Discord answers a missing id with "not snowflake", which names neither the
    tool nor the argument.
  - Added search_messages, wrapping GET /guilds/{guild.id}/messages/search. The
    channel allowlist goes into the query as a multi-valued channel_id filter, so
    search cannot reach further than fetch_messages. Discord returns 25 results
    per request; a larger limit is paged, and offset continues an earlier call.
  - fetch_messages now reports embed content, thread name, the referenced
    message id, and attachment filenames and content types, rather than the
    message text and an attachment count alone.
  - Added delete_message and pin_message.
  - Added list_forum_tags and set_thread_tags, so a forum thread's tags can be
    read and set by name. set_thread_tags reopens an archived thread to apply
    them and leaves it open.
  - Added list_forum_threads, listing a forum's posts with their status tags,
    author, and when each was opened and last active. Upstream can only see a
    post it was sent a message from.
  - Added create_forum_post, opening a post with a title, an opening message and
    optional tags. Nothing upstream or in this fork could open one, so filing a
    report was a request to a human rather than something the bridge did.
  - Added close_thread, setting a thread's archived, locked and pinned state.
    Asking for a state a thread already holds is a no-op rather than an error.
  - Added forward_message, wrapping message_reference type 1 (FORWARD) so a
    report reaches an escalation channel with its attachments and embeds intact
    instead of being retyped as text.
  - fetch_messages pages past the 100-per-request cap Discord enforces, and
    takes a before cursor.
  - The inbound envelope carries the thread name and parent id. A tracker keeps
    its report id in the post title and nowhere else in the payload.
  - Writes a gateway state file (status, ping, last inbound event) so a
    supervisor can tell a deaf connection from a quiet channel without opening
    a second gateway.
  - Messages from other apps and webhooks are delivered rather than dropped;
    only this bot's own messages are skipped. Release and issue activity
    arrives by webhook, and upstream ignored all of it.
  - An inbound message's embeds are flattened into the content it delivers, as
    [embed: …]. An app or webhook leaves content empty and puts the message in
    an embed, so the session was handed a blank message and had to go back for
    the history to read it.
  - Added ensure_forum_tags, which adds tags a forum lacks and sends the
    existing ones back with them, since the underlying call replaces the list.
  - Relays reactions, content edits, deletions, member joins, leaves, role and
    nickname changes, and voice joins, leaves and moves, one line each on the
    same notification channel as a message. Upstream connects with the message
    intents alone and sees none of it.
  - A chunked voice note is transcribed in one helper process rather than one
    per chunk, so the model is loaded once instead of once a chunk. The helper
    flushes a line per chunk, and the bound scales with the chunk count, so a
    stall keeps whatever finished before it and says the note was cut short.
  - Inbound messages are handled one at a time per channel, so they reach the
    session in the order they were sent. Handling is asynchronous and a voice
    note takes far longer to transcribe than a short message takes to arrive,
    so without this a later message overtakes an earlier one.
  - The typing indicator holds its first pulse for a few seconds, so a turn that
    finishes quickly raises no indicator. Discord cannot cancel a pulse once
    sent, and one raised for a two-second reply outlives the reply.
  - Those relays follow the channel's delivery mode rather than its membership
    alone. A channel that delivers every message keeps every event; a
    mention-only channel relays a reaction on the session's own message and
    nothing else; a channel with no groups entry relays nothing, as before.
  - Added list_members, listing a guild's members with presence, roles and
    nickname. The GuildPresences intent is enabled to populate it; presenceUpdate
    is never relayed, since it fires on every status flicker of every member.
  - Added open_dm, opening a DM channel with a member and returning its id, so a
    conversation can be started rather than only answered. Upstream's reply takes
    a channel id and nothing creates one, so a member who has not written first
    is unreachable. Refuses a bot target, which Discord rejects with 50007.
  - Added defaultPolicy, the access policy for guild channels with no groups
    entry. It defaults to requireMention, so a mention reaches the session from
    any channel the bot can see; null restores upstream's drop-unless-listed.
  - Added dmPolicy "guild", accepting a DM from anyone who shares a guild with
    the bot, and dmMirrorChannelId, which copies every accepted DM to a channel.
    dmMirrorExclude lists user IDs whose DMs are never copied, so the operator's
    own messages do not become durable posts in that channel. readAccessFile
    copies it, without which the field parses and is then discarded.
  - gateway.state carries the writing process's pid. Several bridges can hold a
    gateway on one token, and a reader cannot otherwise tell whose heartbeat it
    is looking at.
    The inbound envelope marks a DM with is_dm.
  - Inbound voice notes are transcribed and the text is appended to the message
    content as [voice note: "…"], with transcript in the envelope meta. Upstream
    relays the ogg as an attachment name, so the content is unreadable. The
    transcriber is an external command set by DISCORD_TRANSCRIBER. A note longer
    than one transcriber run is split with ffmpeg into pieces that are
    transcribed in sequence and joined, so its length is not a ceiling and a
    piece that fails leaves a [transcription failed] gap rather than dropping
    the whole note, named so it cannot be read as a pause in the speech. A
    total failure appends [voice note: could not be transcribed: <reason>] and
    the message is delivered regardless.
  - The client is constructed with allowedMentions parse ['users'], so text alone
    never reaches @everyone, @here or a role. reply takes a mentions parameter
    naming exactly what one message may ping ("everyone", "here", "role:<id>");
    parse never carries 'roles', which would permit every role named in the text.
  - A deleted message takes any attachment already downloaded from it. Upstream
    keeps the file: a credential pasted and withdrawn thirty seconds later stayed
    on disk with nothing able to remove it.
  - A reply notifies the person being replied to, once, on the chunk carrying
    the reference. Upstream suppresses that notification for every message.
  - Registers "Report as bug" and "Report as feature request" message
    context-menu commands and relays the right-clicked message to the session as
    a report event, carrying report_kind. The interaction is deferred immediately
    and the outcome is sent as an ordinary channel message, since a session
    outlives the fifteen-minute interaction token.

  - Added describe_server, reporting a guild's channels and categories with their
    ids and types, its roles with position and colour, its custom emoji and its
    member count. Nothing here could report what channels exist, so a channel
    nobody had sent a message from was unreachable. The answer is cached for a
    few minutes, since channel and role lists change rarely.
  - reply takes a poll parameter, attaching a native Discord poll: a question,
    2 to 10 answers, a duration in hours and a multiselect flag. Counts, lengths
    and duration are checked before the send, so a bad poll says what is wrong
    rather than returning a Discord 400. Sending one schedules a reminder at the
    poll's expiry, since Discord reports nothing when a poll closes. That
    reminder is delivered into the session rather than posted: it is sent as the
    bot, and a bot's own messages never return through messageCreate, so a
    posted one reached nobody. It falls back to posting if delivery fails, which
    is what makes an empty channel mean it arrived. A remind a person sets is
    unchanged and still posts.
  - fetch_messages accepts a message link (https://discord.com/channels/…)
    wherever it takes a channel, returning the linked message with the
    conversation either side of it. A link resolves to ids and then goes through
    the same allowlist check as a channel id, so pasting one cannot reach a
    channel that reading would refuse.
  - The typing indicator is held for the length of a turn rather than the ten
    seconds a single sendTyping lasts. It starts only when the message is directed
    at the bot — a mention, a DM, or a reply to something it said — so a message
    read as channel context does not show the bot typing. It re-sends on a timer
    and stops when the reply goes out. There is one timer per channel, each
    carries an absolute deadline that ends it, and a failing pulse clears it. The
    deadline is 20 seconds: it reports that the message was seen without promising
    a reply that may not come. A pulse re-checks the timer after its channel
    lookup, so one already in flight when the reply lands is dropped, and the
    timer is cleared before the reply is sent rather than after it, since Discord
    has no way to cancel an indicator once a pulse has gone.
  - Added remind, scheduling a message the bridge posts to a channel later, with
    list and cancel. Discord has no reminder API: the store is reminders.json
    next to access.json, a due time is read from a relative form ("in 2 hours"),
    a day and time ("friday 9am") or an absolute timestamp, and a minute tick
    posts what is due. A corrupt store is moved aside on read rather than
    stopping the process, and a due time that passed while the bridge was down
    fires on reconnect.
  - Text a Discord user reads names the bot by its own display name rather than
    naming Claude, since the same code runs under whatever name the application
    was given. The pairing instruction keeps the product name, because it names
    the program the operator has to open.

  - Inbound replies carry the message they answer: author and content, not just
    the reference id, so a quoted report is readable without a second lookup. The
    quoted text runs to 1500 characters, enough for the whole of a normal message
    rather than its first line. When the quoted message is itself a reply, its
    own parent is surfaced one level further, so a nested reply is not read
    without the message it answers; resolution stops after that one extra hop.
  - History lines from fetch_messages and search_messages name their sender as
    "username (id)". A live message carries user_id in the envelope; upstream's
    history formatters read author.id and then emitted the display name alone,
    so nothing read back could be checked against a known user id.
  - Mentions are delivered as names rather than ids. Content carries the resolved
    form of a user, role or channel mention, and the ids travel in the envelope
    meta as mentioned_users, mentioned_roles and mentioned_channels, since a tool
    call and a mention back both need an id.
  - A forwarded message is read from its message_snapshots and delivered as
    [forwarded: …], with the origin as forwarded_from_message_id and
    forwarded_from_chat_id. A forward leaves content empty, so upstream and this
    fork both delivered it as a blank message.
  - A components-v2 message is read from its component tree and delivered as
    [components: …]. Such a message carries no content and no embeds, so every
    text path stopped before reaching what it says. Text displays are collected
    in order and control labels follow them; recursion is depth-capped.
  - Polls and stickers on an inbound message are rendered as [poll: question —
    answer (votes)] and [sticker: name]. Both leave content empty.
  - Failures are appended to errors.log next to access.json as well as written to
    stderr. The bridge runs with its stderr held by whatever launched it, so a
    throw while handling an inbound message is unreadable from any session; a
    file is the only copy either side can open. It is capped and truncated at
    256 KB.

  - reply reports what Discord stored rather than what was sent: the mentions
    that actually resolved, and the attachment and embed counts. A bare @name
    that resolved nobody renders as grey text and pings no one, and the sender
    has no other way to see it. Re-serialising the outgoing payload would agree
    with the sender every time, so only the echoed message can disagree. A
    warning is added when the text carries a mention shape and nothing resolved.
  - Deliveries are appended to delivery.log next to errors.log. An MCP result is
    returned once and then gone, so a message that was sent, rendered wrongly or
    later deleted otherwise leaves nothing behind. Capped and truncated at 256 KB.
  - reply qualifies its result when the gateway looks unhealthy: not ready, or no
    inbound traffic for fifteen minutes. A disconnected socket makes send throw,
    which is loud; a socket that is up and has heard nothing sends without error.

Upstream carries no NOTICE file; this one exists to record the above.
